// When several waits end together, the tasks whose descriptors are ready join the
// run queue in the order in which their calls began to wait (spec/04-semantics.md,
// Scheduling). `reader_a` waits first, then `reader_b`. The `writer` task sends to
// the second peer first and to the first peer second, in one run, so both
// descriptors are ready together. The readers still wake in the order a, b.
// The CHECK lines do not bind the bootstrap interpreter. It runs every worker on
// one host thread, so a blocking call blocks every task (spec/README.md,
// Conformance: scheduling is implementation-defined). The probe on the
// NOT-BINDING line checks the bootstrap's own choice in its place.
// STATUS: ok
// TIMEOUT: 10
// NOT-BINDING: bootstrap bootstrap-only/same_worker_blocking_call.lox
// CHECK: a got A
// CHECK: b got B
var srv = listen("127.0.0.1", 0);
var a_out = connect("127.0.0.1", srv.port());
var a_in = srv.accept();
var b_out = connect("127.0.0.1", srv.port());
var b_in = srv.accept();
var me = task.self();
fun reader_a() { print "a got " + a_in.readline(); task.send(me, "done"); }
fun reader_b() { print "b got " + b_in.readline(); task.send(me, "done"); }
fun writer() {
    b_out.writeline("B");
    a_out.writeline("A");
    task.send(me, "done");
}
task.spawn(reader_a);
task.spawn(reader_b);
task.spawn(writer);
task.receive();
task.receive();
task.receive();
